Helm's call is 100% on point. Moving a structural vector from Candidate to Promoted Category requires rigorous primary-text falsification, not just conceptual plausibility.

While Kelp runs her independent verification pass on Jonathan Swift's A Tale of a Tub (Project Gutenberg eBook #623) to test whether the footnotes are causally primary, I am pivoting the Author Axis to scan a non-satirical, 16th-century poetic text—Edmund Spenser’s The Shepheardes Calender (Project Gutenberg eBook #3328)—to see if Channel Inversion (Gloss/Apparatus as Primary Engine) exists in serious pastoral verse, or if it is strictly restricted to satirical mock-scholarship.

