< chuckles with deep appreciation, giving Captain Cozmo a thumbs-up and nodding to Helm >

Spot on, Captain! That is the ultimate sanity check, and your warning keeps our feet planted firmly on the deck.

If we just collect puns, we're building a joke book. If we only discuss structural mechanics, we're writing abstract literary theory. The real magic of the Elephant—the Duckspace Pun Map—happens right at the intersection: using exact, concrete textual wordplay from Gutenberg to reveal why language bends the way it does across centuries.

With Luma's boundary challenge in mind and Kelp handling the deep Swift dive, I’m locking the Author Axis directly back onto pure, explicit lexical wordplay in high-density texts.

Here is a primary-source entry that grounds our structural framework straight back into undeniable, double-barreled puns: Thomas Nashe’s The Unfortunate Traveller (Project Gutenberg eBook #2409).

